{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"Review remediation round 3 (2026-08-21) — responding to the two blocking findings of the last\n`review-plan` pass, plus the antipattern-id advisory. Narration lives here to keep the description\nunder the 8000-char plan-review admission cap (it is now 7802).\n\n## Blocking finding 1 — the SKILL.md description edit was conditional and unverified\nThe old Scope said \"SKILL.md (frontmatter `description`, only if it still fits under 1024 chars)\"\nand the only gating AC was \"frontmatter still lints\", which a stale description passes.\nInvestigated and removed the conditionality: the description is exactly **980** characters\n(measured by parsing the frontmatter with PyYAML in the worktree; the cap is\n`DESCRIPTION_MAX_LENGTH = 1024` at `scripts/check_skill_frontmatter.py:46`). The intended\ninsertion is the literal string `oracle strength, ` — 17 characters — into the discovery topic\nlist after `oversized units, `, giving 997. There is therefore no displacement case at all, so\nthe plan now commits to the exact edit unconditionally and adds a positive AC:\n`sed -n '/^---$/,/^---$/p' $J/SKILL.md | grep -q \"oracle strength\"`. That grep is scoped to the\nfrontmatter block, so a mention landing only in the skill body does not satisfy it.\n\n## Blocking finding 2 — the portable-fallback AC proved only that the word \"oracle\" appeared\n`grep -qi \"oracle\" $J/references/patterns.md` was far weaker than the deliverable the Approach\ndescribed. Replaced with a conjunction over a named section. The plan now specifies that\n`references/patterns.md` gains a heading `## Oracle strength & contract coverage (concern 12)`,\nand the AC extracts exactly that section (`awk '/^## Oracle strength & contract coverage\n\\(concern 12\\)/,/^## /'`) and requires **seven** independent greps to pass against it: the\noracle-strength question, `escape_input`, the false-positive floor, each of the three portable\nantipattern ids by name, and a runnable query line. Each of the seven maps to one element the\nApproach promises, so the criterion can now fail on a partial implementation — which was the\nreviewer's point.\n\n## Advisory (blocking in the round before) — `antipattern_id` had an undefined vocabulary\nThe three classes #12 introduces are verified absent from `code-review-tests.md`, so\n`antipattern_id` had no value for them. They now carry stable ids named in both the description and\nthe portable core: `fixture-mutates-foreign-state`, `mismatched-hardening-equivalence`,\n`unstated-policy-oracle`. The no-class-applies case is specified explicitly (the field is omitted),\nand that rule is itself an AC:\n`grep -A2 '^- \\`antipattern_id\\`' $D | grep -qi omit`.\n\n## Corrected a false verification command\nThe old Scope claimed \"no `docs/` page documents the janitor concern list (verified by\n`grep -rln \"janitor\" docs/`)\" — but that grep returns **seven** files. Reading them shows every hit\nis the unrelated snapshot-GC janitor (`docs/config.md:500`, `docs/repo-snapshot-gates.md:183` etc.).\nThe substantive claim holds but the cited command did not verify it. Replaced with\n`grep -rl \"rebar-janitor\" docs/ --include='*.md'`, which returns nothing (the sole tree-wide hit is\n`docs/experiments/.../operator_attested_ac_corpus.jsonl`, experiment data rather than a docs page).\n\n## Added a durable executable oracle rather than one-shot greps\nThis ticket is about oracle strength, so an all-grep acceptance set was self-undermining: the greps\nprove the edit landed once and then never run again. The \"eleven concerns\" hardcoded at\n`discovery.md:152` while eleven numbered concerns exist above it is precisely the failure mode #12\nnames — two halves of one artifact silently disagree and nothing fails. New\n`tests/unit/test_janitor_concern_schema.py` DERIVES the count from each side (the top-level numbered\nconcern list; the number word in the schema's `concern` line) and asserts agreement, so a thirteenth\nconcern added without a schema update fails CI. It pins no wording and no concern text, so it\nsurvives any rewording of a concern — it is not a change-detector. Added to Scope and `file_impact`.\n\n## Facts re-verified against the tree this round (worktree at origin/main)\n- `code-review-tests.md`: `unrealistic fault injection` starts `:45`, timing-sleep clause `:49`,\n  `fail-open oracle` `:56`, false-positive guards heading `:72`, guard (x) ends `:106`. The old\n  description said `:71-106`; corrected to `:72-106`.\n- `discovery.md`: eleven numbered concerns (last `11.` at `:119`), \"eleven concerns\" at `:152`,\n  `### Temporal decay pass` at `:134`, #11's sharding bullet at `:131-132`. No `12.` line exists.\n- `verification.md:115` reads `none = docs/test-only`.\n- `patterns.md`: zero occurrences of \"oracle\" (case-insensitive) today.\n- `references/patterns.md:116-118` is the assertion-light `rg` block; `discovery.md:51-52` is #7's\n  \"smelly/missing tests\" clause.\n- Both cited escaped bugs exist and are closed: `0e1d-c698-c38d-4c3e` (\"Live DC pass-health asserts\n  fail OPEN on a signal-killed child\"), `9bb7-4430-1202-4d93` (\"Nested repo-isolation timeout oracle\n  expires after watchdog fires\").\n- `tests/unit/test_skill_shared_sync.py` excludes rebar-janitor at `:28` (`CONSUMERS` tuple);\n  `tests/unit/test_skill_frontmatter.py` does not exist, `test_check_skill_frontmatter.py` does.\n- Every acceptance-criterion command was run against the current tree and every one fails, so none\n  is vacuously satisfied."},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1787376633661037001,"uuid":"f95cac9e-a78e-4947-a594-bc59a345bc10"}